  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern today is defined a trough of \nlow pressure across the Intermountain West/Northern Plains with \na low center over Wyoming, a second trough west of California \ncentered near 35N/129W and a dampening ridge of high pressure \nover the SERN states with a high center over ERN TX/WRN LA. In \nthe lower-to-middle levels of the atmosphere (850 to 700mb), \nthere is an weak trough moving into southwest Arizona from the \nnorthern Baja Mexico.\n\nThe latest GOES precipitable water imagery indicated that the weak\nlower-level feature has imported some moisture into Southeast \nArizona from the SW. Precipitable water values between 0.80 to \n1.00 inches extend across Pima/SE Pinal and Santa Cruz counties,\nwith lower values between 0.65 and 0.80 inches east of Tucson.\nAs this feature ejects across southern Arizona today, we should\nhave enough lower-level moisture convergence to support a weak \nshower to develop across south-central Arizona. I still wouldn't\nexpect anything strong given the shortwave ridging occuring in \nthe middle to upper levels, but since the sub-cloud layer is \ndry, we may see some gusty/breezy winds to 35 mph accompany the \nlight shower downdrafts. \n\nMonday, both of the upper troughs continue to track east with\none centered over the upper Midwest and the other along the\ncentral California coastline. An amplified shortwave ridge\nbuilding between these two features into north-central Mexico \nwill result in a southerly flow across Sonora/Chihuahua MX. This\nwill draw deeper moisture starting Monday. The bulk of the \nmoisture will advect into New Mexico and far eastern portions of\nArizona Tuesday and Wednesday. The best deformation aloft/lift \nbetween the upper trough to our west and the subtropical ridge \nto our east will occur over New Mexico. However, there appears \nto be a westward expansion of this area of lift into our neck of\nthe woods. This should increase the potential for heavy \nrainfall across Southeast Arizona from Tucson east on both \nTuesday and Wednesday. In fact, the latest NBM suggests there is\na 10 percent chance of rainfall totals greater than 1.25 inches\nalong the Arizona/New Mexico border between 12Z Tuesday and 12Z\nWednesday. \n\nThe NHC still identifies an area 350 miles south of Manzanillo\nMexico (Invest 99E) with conditions conducive for it to become a\ntropical depression or storm within the next day or so. There \nis still a ton of uncertainty with the forecast track and \nstrength of this feature. In a general sense, it will work its \nway northwest to a location west of the tip of the Baja between \n200-240 hours. My intuition tells me that we will see a broad \nridge develop over northwest Mexico that will block the deeper \nmoisture and force it to track farther west where moisture \neventually shears out to the NE across the Desert Southwest. We\nwill see...there is a lot of time to monitor its development.",
